
Incident Report - Network Attack Detection
__Executive Summary__
A simulated SSH-based attack was conducted against a Cowrie honeypot deployed on a seperate virtual machine. The objective was to observe attacker behavior, capture network traffic, and analyze authentication attempts. The honeypot successfully detected and logged the activity, demonstrating effective attack visibility and monitoring.
Attack Description
Install Kali Linux and Cowrie Honeypot on separate VMs. The attacker system running Kali Linux performed reconnaissance and attempted SSH access to the honeypot over TCP port 2222. The attacker used the username 'root' with a password-based authentication attempt (Note: could also logon without a password). The honeypot accepted the credentials and provided a simulated shell environment to observe post-authentication behavior.
Indicators of Compromise (IoCs)
Attacker IP Address: 10.0.2.7
Target TCP Port: 2222
Protocol: SSH
Username Used: root
Authentication Method: Password
Evidence Collected
• Network traffic captured using Wireshark (cowrie_attack.pcap)
• Cowrie logs showing SSH handshake, authentication attempt, shell access, and session termination
• Use Nmap to perform scanning and basic attacks. Nmap scan results confirming exposed SSH service
Mitigation & Recommendations
• Disable direct root SSH access on production systems
• Enforce strong authentication mechanisms such as key-based authentication and MFA
• Apply firewall rules to restrict access to management ports
• Deploy IDS/IPS and honeypots for early threat detection
• Continuously monitor authentication logs for suspicious activity
Of course, when we deploy a honeypot for this purpose, it’s recommended to have it in an air-gapped environment or DMV so we don’t give the attackers the chance of crossing over to our trusted network zone.


